    <estimated-effort>12-18 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 27 C4 research, "Access-permission misalignments" 1-8 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md:65-73). Owner round-2 decisions 3 (Restricted vs Secure clarified) and Q6 (communications inherit the parent's permission; their own field is retired).</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>The record-level Access Permission choice (Standard / Limited / Restricted on sprk_project, sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment) is half-implemented. Restricted is enforced only at read time on the workforce plane. Limited does nothing anywhere. The Manage Access dialog contradicts itself on Restricted, and sprk_communication carries its own copy of the field that nothing enforces. Make all three levels mean exactly what the owner decided, at read time and at write time, on every plane. Then retire the communication copy in favour of inheriting the parent's permission.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer: the unified evaluator (AccessibleRecordSetService), the external-grant write path, and the shared Fluent v9 AccessGrantModal. Follow ADRs strictly. An ADR contract change here is path B, surfaced, not implemented silently.</role>

  <goal>After this task:
(1) Standard admits every grant type.
(2) Limited admits only named, direct contact grants. Org-inherited grants, standing-grant membership and org-expansion contribute nothing. This reuses the FR-22 Secure pre-max suppression, on the workforce contact plane, the systemuser plane's contact-grants term, and the CIAM plane.
(3) A Secure record behaves as Limited for contacts. Where both flags are present, Restricted wins.
(4) Restricted removes all contact-sourced access and leaves internal (systemuser) access untouched.
(5) The grant endpoints refuse, BEFORE any side effect and with a readable ProblemDetails message rather than a bare 500:
  - contact and organization grants on Restricted records;
  - organization-wide grants on Secure or Limited records.
(6) The Manage Access dialog hides the options that do not apply, and keeps "+ User" enabled on Restricted.
(7) The access-permission pill honours the control's disabled state.
(8) No code reads or writes sprk_communication.sprk_accesspermission. An operator-run, dry-run-default script retires the column.
(9) ADR-003's evaluator contract is amended to name Limited alongside Secure.
(10) Every one of the above is proven live on spaarkedev1 at a manual gate.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>THE OWNER'S MODEL (round 2, 2026-09-30, binding; notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md:20-22):
- **Restricted** is a grant-access level on ANY record. It means no contact-based (non-systemuser) access at all, while internal users are unaffected. This includes workforce contact-only callers (Type-2 unlicensed employees) and CIAM contacts.
- **Secure** is a separate flag. A secure record MAY have contact access as long as it is not Restricted, but only named, direct grants count. That is why Secure implies Limited for contacts.
- **Limited** is what teams-app-r1 FR-14 "Option A" always said it was (projects/teams-app-r1/spec.md:63, :180): named grants only, no standing and no auto grants. Its meaning is exactly what FR-22 Secure suppression already does.
- A Write-holder changing sprk_accesspermission and then adding external users is ACCEPTABLE (owner, C4). Do not lock or FLS the field; that is the separate sprk_issecure decision owned by another task.

THE EIGHT MISALIGNMENTS (verified 2026-09-30 against current code):
1. **Limited does nothing anywhere.** RootRecordFlags carries only IsSecure and IsRestricted (Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalCallerContext.cs:139-153). ExternalParticipationService reads sprk_accesspermission but only compares it to Restricted (ExternalParticipationService.cs:521, :574, :603-608). The evaluator's suppression predicate is IsSecure only (AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1042 systemuser plane, :1258 contact plane). It feeds GrantedRightsFor (:238-256: isSecure ? DirectAccessLevel : AccessLevel), the standing term (:1270-1277) and the org-expansion term (:1283-1290).
2. **Limited's intended meaning is the FR-22 path above.** Extend the predicate; do not build a second one.
3. **The dialog contradicts itself on Restricted.**
   - `grantsBlocked = accessPermissionState === 'restricted'` (Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/AccessGrantModal/AccessGrantModal.tsx:538) feeds actionsBlocked (:1083).
   - actionsBlocked disables "+ Contact" (:1197), "+ Organization" (:1211), "+ User" (:1225), every row checkbox (:1248), the level dropdown (:1284) and Add (:1307).
   - Meanwhile the banner (:1146-1153) says system users may have access, and the server's Restricted veto keeps internal access (AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1044-1051 membership term survives; the veto is at :393). "+ User" is an internal POA share and must stay enabled.
4. **Restricted is not checked at grant-WRITE time.**
   - /grant validates the grantee kind, the root, the level and the expiry only (GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:84-103), then upserts (:186-383).
   - /invite-and-grant onboards a CIAM account BEFORE it grants (InviteAndGrantExternalUserEndpoint.cs:92-108), so a refused grant must be refused before :92.
   - /invite (InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs:39) creates a contact and a CIAM identity against a named root with no grant at all.
   - On CIAM, Restricted is not applied at READ time either. That is C1, owned by task 135, which this task depends on.
5. **The dialog allows "+ Organization" on secure records.** The workforce plane suppresses such grants; before task 135, the CIAM plane honours them.
6. **The Manage Access gate ignores both flags** (RecordAccessGateEndpoint.cs:105-107). This is BY DESIGN. The gate answers the delegation question (Write) and task 139 keeps it that way. The flags govern WHICH grant types apply, and they are enforced at write time here. Record that reasoning in the endpoint remarks. Do not change the gate.
7. **sprk_communication has its own sprk_accesspermission that the BFF never reads.** RootFlagSources lists only the three roots (ExternalParticipationService.cs:502-508), and any other entity type returns "no vetoes" at :549-553. Readers and writers found 2026-09-30:
   - EmailWorkspace.mapping.ts:30-55 (EMAIL_TRACKING_FIELDS.accessPermission, DEFAULT_ACCESS_PERMISSION_OPTIONS), :220 and :247 (mapped into recordState);
   - useEmailWorkspaceRecord.ts:40, :115-118, :129 (updateAccessPermission writes the column);
   - EmailWorkspace.types.ts:87 and EmailWorkspace/index.ts:15 (the options export);
   - EmailTrackingPanel.tsx:41-48, :78, :95-103 and EmailAssociationsAndTracking.types.ts:121-132 (renders and changes it);
   - the TrackingFieldTrio PCF's comments that call these values "sprk_communication" values (pcf/TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:15, :170-175). The same integers are verified live on the three roots.
   Owner Q6: communications inherit the parent's access permission. Their own field is retired.
8. **The access-permission pill is never disabled** (Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/TrackingFieldTrio/TrackingFieldTrio.tsx:335-367). A user on a read-only form is offered a menu whose write then fails. The shared component has no disabled/read-only prop at all, and the PCF never reads `context.mode.isControlDisabled` or the bound attribute's `security.editable`, so there is nothing to wire yet. Its siblings in the same row share the defect: the Monitor Switch (:315-320) and the High Priority Switch (:323-328) also ignore a read-only form.

STANDING GRANTS ARE SUBJECT-LEVEL, NOT PER-RECORD.
- contact.sprk_standinggrant is written on the Contact, not on the record. sprk_organization.sprk_standinggrant is written on the organization.
- ⚠️ The modal has NO standing-grant control any more. `onSetStandingGrant` stays in the props contract but is unused (AccessGrantModal.tsx:524-525, task 073 UAT v1.0.24 #5: "the standing grant is now set on the Contact record itself"); the PCF still passes a handler (pcf/TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:978, :1200) that nothing calls. The module doc's claim that "Limited ... hides the standing-grant option" (AccessGrantModal.tsx:58-59) describes nothing the code does. So there is nothing to "hide" in the modal, and no per-record standing write to refuse.
- On Secure/Limited/Restricted, the standing term is suppressed at READ time (item 1). What the modal still shows is standing ROWS in Current Access (AccessGrantModal.tsx:658-667, :1327-1369, labelled "Standing grant — ongoing access to assigned records"). On such records those rows confer nothing. Rendering them as suppressed is task 066's scope (Current Access provenance + suppressed rendering, FR-30), and 066 as written names only Secure and Restricted. The main session must amend 066 (and its report source, 064) to include Limited; this task supplies the Limited signal they consume.

LIVE FACTS (session 27): Standard 100000000 / Limited 100000001 / Restricted 100000002 are identical on sprk_project, sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment. No FLS covers sprk_accesspermission or sprk_issecure (0 fieldpermission rows).</background>
